  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Considering PulseLive Architecture**\n\nOkay, I\'m diving into the architecture for \"PulseLive.\" Right now, I\'m focusing on the \"Presenter View,\" specifically how session creation will work. The plan is to have a dynamic, short room code generated, either as random numbers, words, or alphanumeric strings. This ensures quick session creation.\n\n\n**Refining Session Management**\n\nI\'ve decided on a 4 or 5-digit uppercase alphanumeric code for sessions, it\'s very clean! Now I\'m fleshing out session management. I\'m thinking of multi-choice polls, with active/closed toggles and real-time results display (animated charts or percentages). Also, a Q&A section with upvote-ordered questions, moderated with \"answered\" and \"archived\" toggles. Plus, a QR code for direct audience joining and a clean display link.\n\n\n**Analyzing Backend Strategies**\n\nOkay, I\'m now exploring the backend service (`server.ts`). Considering real-time capabilities, I\'m leaning toward an Express server. Initially, I thought about in-memory storage for rooms, polls, and questions, which I like for speed, but I need to make sure I am robust. I\'ve now decided against SSE. HTTP polling is a very robust option that is easy to implement in an iframe, and doesn\'t face the connection issues that WebSockets or SSE might.\n\n\n**Prioritizing Polling Strategies**\n\nI am now focusing on the optimal real-time communication strategy. After more analysis, I am leaning toward highly-optimized short-polling (every 1.5 seconds for presenters, every 2 seconds for audience members). This option offers extreme reliability, easy implementation, and robust behavior in any network environment. I am also planning a fallback strategy, although short-polling should be sufficient. I am now working on the data structures. The `Room` will include the room code, title, creation date, polls, and questions, and `Poll` will include its ID, question, options, and votes.\n\n\n**Defining Data Structures**\n\nI\'ve fleshed out the data structure details for polls and questions, including `votedUsers`, `upvotedBy`, and timestamps. Now I am thinking about how to generate QR codes. I\'m choosing a free public API for QR code generation; it is incredibly easy and lightweight, requires no extra dependencies, and is very reliable!\n\n\n"
    modified_response: "I will update the scripts in `package.json` to support a full-stack Express + Vite architecture, compiling the backend to a bundle using `esbuild`."
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 9
      nanos: 460893742
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
