  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Designing the PulseLive App**\n\nI\'ve got `server.ts` up and running, and I\'m moving on to crafting the interface in `/src/App.tsx`. I\'m starting to visualize the PulseLive layout and screens, and I\'m focusing on a highly polished, single-page-app routing system within that file.\n\n\n**Refining App Routing**\n\nI\'ve decided to manage routing using standard React state, leveraging `window.location.hash` for simplicity and compatibility within the Vite SPA. I\'m building a hook or component to parse the hash dynamically. Now I\'m focusing on the initial landing page design, aiming for a premium feel with the title \"PulseLive\" and the subtitle \"Lightweight, real-time polling and Q&A. Engage.\"\n\n\n**Defining User Interaction Flow**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the initial user flows. For a presenter, I\'m setting up a \"Create Session\" flow, letting them name the session, leading to a Presenter Dashboard. Participants can use a 5-letter code to join. A \"Demo Room\" button will provide an interactive preview. Now I\'m starting to design the presenter view, the layout, including a room code display and a QR code integration using a service for QR generation. The join URL needs to be constructed.\n\n\n**Considering Join URL Format**\n\nI\'m evaluating the optimal format for join URLs. I\'m leaning toward hash routing, specifically `/#/room/${roomId}`, for direct QR code compatibility. This simplifies the user experience. I\'m also considering other query string formats and state-based join URLs. I need to ensure a robust solution that accommodates various use cases.\n\n**Evaluating Charting Library**\n\nNow, I\'m assessing the chart rendering. I\'m exploring `recharts` to build a presentation-grade, animated, and responsive bar chart, as the guidance suggests. I\'ve confirmed `recharts` isn\'t currently installed but can be added. I\'m now debating whether to use `recharts` or design a custom-styled chart.\n\n\n**Prioritizing Custom Chart**\n\nI\'ve decided on a custom Tailwind bar chart with Framer Motion for optimal control and aesthetic appeal. I\'m prioritizing building a beautiful, responsive, and animated bar chart. The goal is a sleek, premium design with specific color schemes and glowing effects. I\'m confirming correct Framer Motion import from `\'motion/react\'` to ensure smooth integration and compatibility. I\'ve verified the installed package version is `motion: ^12.23.24`.\n\n\n**Confirming Motion Library**\n\nI\'m now implementing the custom animated charts as prioritized, and decided that importing from `motion/react` is the correct approach for animations, to satisfy React guidelines. The `motion.div` is used to animate the horizontal bars using Framer Motion. I\'m focusing on rendering horizontal bars proportional to vote percentages, with sleek vote count badges and a gradient transition. The code is coming together using `initial={{ width: 0 }} animate={{ width: \\`${percentage}%\\` }}`. I\'m moving on to the Presenter Dashboard design.\n\n\n**Designing Presenter Control Panel**\n\nI\'m now expanding the Presenter Dashboard, focusing on the Control Panel tab design. I\'m visualizing the UI for poll creation: question text input, option fields (with add/remove buttons, minimum of two required), and poll controls (activate, close, delete). I\'m also planning the Q&A moderation features: displaying questions (sorted by upvotes), marking as \"Answered,\" archiving/deleting questions, and a \"Clear All\" option. I\'m leaning toward a tabbed interface for a clean split between control panel and projector view.\n\n\n**Defining Projector Mode Design**\n\nI\'m now implementing the \"Projector Mode\" view for PulseLive, a key component for sharing sessions. This will display a fullscreen optimized view for the audience with the active poll chart on the left, and Q&A on the right, plus a prominent QR code and join instructions at the top. I\'ve designed a single presenter view that features the Projector Mode button! I\'m making sure that changes are automatically reflected across all open tabs through backend syncing.\n\n\n**Defining URL and User ID**\n\nI\'m now nailing down the URL structure: `/ #/` for landing, `/#/room/:id` for participant view, `/#/room/:id/presenter` for presenter control, and `/#/room/:id/projector` for the full-screen view. I\'m also addressing anonymous user IDs by storing a unique identifier in `localStorage`. This is going to be used for voting and Q&A to provide single-vote rules and simple implementation.\n\n\n\n**Deciding Theme Strategy**\n\nI\'m now weighing whether to implement a dark or light theme. I\'m focusing on the design guidelines, which suggest prioritizing a clean, high-contrast light theme with rich indigo/violet accents. I will keep a dark theme ready to deploy if the user explicitly requests one. I think a light theme would be best.\n\n\n**Defining App Styling**\n\nI\'m now prioritizing the visual aesthetic, aiming for a modern, clean design inspired by Linear and Stripe. I\'m focusing on off-white backgrounds, pristine borders, deep charcoal headings, and vibrant indigo accents to achieve a high-contrast, professional look that\'s legible on any device. I\'ll make the styling consistent. I\'m thinking of how I can build modular components.\n\n\\n\\n\n\nI\'m now writing and organizing the frontend code for modularity, following the design guidance. I\'m creating `/src/types.ts` for shared interfaces and types, splitting components into separate files, and extracting data/logic from the main `App.tsx` file to maintain a manageable codebase and avoid token limit issues, as suggested.\n\n\n**Structuring Component Files**\n\n\\n\\n\n\nI\'m now focusing on architecting the component file structure for modularity and maintainability. I\'m creating the following component files: `/src/components/PresenterControl.tsx`, `/src/components/ProjectorView.tsx`, `/src/components/ParticipantView.tsx`, and `/src/components/LandingPage.tsx`, as I had earlier planned. Now, I\'m thinking of creating `/src/utils/api.ts` to manage fetching and requests, and to handle the real-time polling. This should encapsulate all networking logic, as intended.\n\n\n"
    modified_response: "I will create a helper module `src/lib/api.ts` to handle all the REST API communication with the Express server. This includes creating rooms, creating polls, activating/closing polls, voting, asking questions, upvoting, and toggling question statuses. It will also provide helper utilities, such as retrieving/creating the anonymous participant user ID."
